---
import CodeBlock from '../CodeBlock.astro';
import StepCard from '../StepCard.astro';
---

<div class="sec" id="delta-follow-up">
  <p><a href="/#s6">← Back to the main guide</a></p>
  <div class="num">OPTIONAL FOLLOW-UP</div>
  <h1>Review a change in Delta — with help</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Review one small change to your finished website. Do this optional exercise
    with the person helping you; it needs setup the main guide does not cover.
  </p>
  <p>
    The main guide does not create a Git repository, a Git remote, or a
    <code class="i">my-site-delta</code> copy. Delta needs different setup, which
    you will check together below. Do not publish the project just to make this exercise
    work.
  </p>
  <nav class="guide-contents" id="contents" aria-labelledby="contents-title">
    <h2 id="contents-title">On this page</h2>
    <div class="toc">
      <a href="#compatibility">Check compatibility and risks</a>
      <a href="#prepare">1–2. Prepare and open a working copy</a>
      <a href="#review">3. Request and review a change</a>
      <a href="#recovery">If something goes wrong</a>
    </div>
  </nav>
  <h2 id="compatibility">Before installing: compatibility and risks</h2>
  <p>
    Delta's only Mac download is for Apple Silicon on macOS 13 or later; there
    is no Intel Mac version. Check Apple menu → About This Mac before
    installing. The Mac installation currently includes a Terminal command, so
    do that step with the person helping you. On Windows or Linux, check the
    requirements on the linked installation page together.
  </p>
  <p>
    Delta is a collaborative agent workspace built by Zed. It is in public beta:
    you need a Zed account and must accept an early-access agreement, and
    details may change. Its safety model is different. Under Work In, Isolated
    Delta Worktree gives a thread a separate copy of the project's files;
    Existing Local Checkout makes the agent work directly in your selected
    folder. Check the Work In selection: a new thread can inherit your previous
    choice, and the location is locked once the agent has used the project.
  </p>
  <p>
    Neither choice is a security sandbox. Delta's
    <a href="https://delta.dev/docs/privacy-and-security/agentic-safety"
      >safety docs</a
    >
    say it does not sandbox agents: an agent has unrestricted access to the device
    where it runs. Delta's own agent has no permission system and does not ask before
    calling tools, including destructive ones. Asking for a plan is not enforced approval.
    An outside backup helps recovery, but is not protected from an agent with device
    access.
  </p>
  <p>
    Delta can also run Claude Agent, a separate beta agent that uses Claude's
    own permission prompts. That is a different setup from this exercise; do not
    assume those prompts are active unless you and your helper have chosen and
    checked it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Project contents and history <a
      href="https://delta.dev/docs/privacy-and-security/data-storage"
      >sync to Delta's servers</a
    > when you add a project, including tracked files and untracked files Git does
    not ignore. Agent requests may also send relevant messages and file contents to
    your selected model provider, not automatically every file in the repository.
    Use only material you are comfortable uploading. Backups cannot undo disclosure,
    and deleting a thread on your computer does not reliably delete its history from
    Delta's servers. Send redacted error text to the person helping you rather than
    sharing a thread.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are comfortable with those risks and your computer is supported,
    follow Delta's official <a href="https://delta.dev/docs/installation"
      >installation instructions</a
    > with the person helping you, then complete the in-app welcome setup. Check <a
      href="https://delta.dev/docs/agents/models-and-providers">model access</a
    > and costs before paying. Delta's own agent cannot use a Claude subscription.
    A Claude subscription works only through
    <a href="https://delta.dev/docs/agents/claude-agent">Claude Agent</a>, in
    Delta's desktop app, where usage counts against your Claude plan's limits.
    Signing out of Claude Agent also signs you out of Claude Code on the same
    computer.
  </p>
  <h2 id="prepare">Prepare your working copy</h2>
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="1" title={'Create a separate working copy with help'}>
      <p>
        Close Claude Code, Delta, and any other agents first. Find your checked,
        untouched <code class="i">my-site-checked</code> backup in Finder or File
        Explorer. If it is missing or you are unsure it is a good backup, stop and
        ask the person helping you before proceeding.
      </p>
      <p>
        Duplicate that backup and name the new folder <code class="i"
          >my-site-delta</code
        >. Keep the backup outside the new working folder, for example as a
        sibling beside it, not nested inside it. Do not overwrite an existing
        folder with that name. Leave both <code class="i">my-site</code> and
        <code class="i">my-site-checked</code> unchanged, and never open the backup
        as a Delta project.
      </p>
      <p>
        Delta works with Git repositories, folders with saved change history.
        Its <a href="https://delta.dev/docs/concepts/worktrees"
          >workspace docs</a
        > say a project must be the top-level folder of a Git repository with a configured
        remote, another location for that history. If there are several remotes, one
        must be named <code class="i">origin</code>. Ask the person helping you
        to check and prepare this prerequisite on <code class="i"
          >my-site-delta</code
        >, and review what its files and history would upload. If it has only
        local history or no Git setup, stop until they have helped. Do not
        invent a remote, publish files, or ask an agent to set this up for you.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
    <StepCard number="2" title={'Open the working copy'}>
      <p>
        With your helper, check Settings → Thread → Sharing and confirm Default
        Visibility is not set to Your organization. Do not share this exercise's
        thread.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Choose Add Project.</li>
        <li>
          Select the top-level <code class="i">my-site-delta</code> folder, not its
          photos folder.
        </li>
        <li>
          If a Projects Sync to the Cloud notice appears, read it before
          choosing Continue.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Delta's <a
          href="https://delta.dev/docs/getting-started/quick-start#open-your-project"
          >Quick Start</a
        >
        is a reference if the screen differs. This exercise uses the checkout selection
        and request below, not the Quick Start's task.
      </p>
      <p>
        For this exercise, deliberately select Work In → Existing Local
        Checkout. Check the project menu's checkout path points to <code
          class="i">my-site-delta</code
        > before sending anything. This changes that copy directly, not an isolated
        worktree. Open that same folder's <code class="i">index.html</code>
        in your browser. Run only one agent at a time, with no other threads working
        in this checkout. You do not need Git commands for this review.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
  </div>
  <h2 id="review">Request and review one change</h2>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'One small change'}
    code={'Read CLAUDE.md. Add "Maker and photographer" below my name in index.html; use style.css if needed. Change nothing else. Do not run Git commands, publish or push the project anywhere. Use only Delta\'s normal project synchronization. Show a short plan and wait for my ok before editing. Then list changed files and checks.'}
    copy
  />
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="3" title={'Review the result'}>
      <p>
        Read the plan before replying; remember that waiting is a request, not a
        permission control. Open Changes with the upper-right diff icon to
        compare additions and removals (a "diff"). Select a line or passage and
        start typing to add a comment, then submit it, or send a normal message.
        Refresh <code class="i">my-site-delta</code>'s <code class="i"
          >index.html</code
        > in your browser; check the words and narrow layout.
      </p>
      <p>
        In Existing Local Checkout the agent's edits appear in your folder
        directly. Treat them as already made, not awaiting your acceptance.
        Leave the main website and backup alone. For help, send the person
        helping you redacted error text, not a shared thread.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
  </div>
  <h2 id="recovery">If something goes wrong</h2>
  <p>
    Stop and close all agents, including Delta and Claude Code. Keep the damaged
    working folder for inspection; do not delete it or restore affected files
    inside it. With the person helping you, make a fresh working copy from the
    untouched <code class="i">my-site-checked</code> backup, using a new name such
    as <code class="i">my-site-delta-retry-1</code> that does not already exist. Keep
    the backup outside this new folder too. If the backup may have changed, stop rather
    than assuming it is safe.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ask the person helping you to recheck the fresh copy and its Git setup
    before adding it to Delta. Confirm the new checkout path and open the new
    copy in your browser before trying again. Do not resume an old thread
    against the damaged folder or open the backup in Delta.
  </p>
  <nav class="toc" aria-label="After the Delta exercise">
    <a href="/index.html#s7">Main guide: cheat sheet →</a>
    <a href="/publish/index.html">Optional: publish your site →</a>
    <a href="#contents">↑ Back to contents</a>
  </nav>
</div>
